= Quotations <sec-quotes>

#quote(block: true, attribution: [Immanuel Kant])[Have courage to use your own understanding!] <epigraph>

Ezetta knows three kinds of quotations. All three are the same thing in the text, and with the menu "Quotation" in the bar you switch between them.

The *quotation in the text* stands in the middle of a sentence: Kant's motto of the Enlightenment is #quote[Sapere aude] @kant1784[p. 481], and the citation follows right behind it. You do not have to type quotation marks, the language of the project sets them.

The *block quotation* is a longer passage that stands on its own. Its source does not belong inside the quotation but under it:

#quote(block: true, attribution: [@kant1784[p. 481]])[Enlightenment is man's emergence from his self-incurred immaturity.]

When you quote from another language, a *translation* can follow the block quotation: while the cursor is in the quotation, it offers "Add translation" at its end, next to "Add source". The translation has a source of its own, and the template sets it under the original or beside it:

#quote(block: true, attribution: [@kant1784[p. 481]])[Aufklärung ist der Ausgang des Menschen aus seiner selbstverschuldeten Unmündigkeit.]

#quote(block: true, attribution: [(my translation)])[Enlightenment is the human being's emergence from their self-incurred immaturity.] <translation>

The third is the *epigraph* at the beginning of this chapter. It is set apart, and its source is free text, not a citation.
